Title:
Borrowing Windows Hello keys (for authentication and persistence)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post explains how Windows Hello for Business (WHFB) private keys can be “borrowed” from a compromised user session to authenticate to Microsoft Entra ID without needing the user’s PIN/biometrics. It shows two main flows: generating WHFB-signed assertions to request Primary Refresh Tokens (PRTs), and using the same WHFB key as a FIDO2/WebAuthn passkey to obtain tokens or interactive browser sessions. The key insight is that WHFB keys, while TPM-bound, can still be used for SSO in contexts like RDP and via CNG/NCrypt APIs with cached “ticket” behavior, enabling low-priv usage from within the user context. The WebAuthn angle enables phishing-resistant auth compliance and can produce tokens without device claims, which can then be leveraged to register a new device and later mint PRTs for persistence. Tooling and PoC scripts are provided via ROADtools/roadtx, and the post includes a basic KQL detection for WHFB sign-ins lacking device identity. This is primarily useful for red teams/pentesters and Entra ID defenders validating Conditional Access and device-bound assumptions.

Technical Focus:
- Windows Hello for Business (WHFB) key usage and SSO semantics
- CNG/NCrypt (`ncrypt.dll`) and Passport/Platform Key Storage Providers (KSP)
- Primary Refresh Token (PRT) request flow using WHFB-signed JWT assertions
- WebAuthn/FIDO2 assertion generation using WHFB keys (passkey emulation)
- Entra ID device registration and token “upgrade” paths (device claim absence)
- Detection via Entra SigninLogs/KQL (WHFB auth on unmanaged/deviceless sign-ins)

Use Cases:
- Post-compromise cloud pivot: mint PRTs/tokens from a hijacked Windows user session
- Persistence: register attacker-controlled Entra device, then obtain renewable PRTs
- Bypass assumptions around “TPM-bound == non-reusable” for cloud authentication
- Validate Conditional Access controls (device compliance requirements vs phishing-resistant auth)
- Blue team hunting for anomalous WHFB usage without device identifiers

Keywords:
Windows Hello for Business, WHFB, Microsoft Entra ID, Azure AD, PRT, Primary Refresh Token, SSO, TPM, CNG, NCryptOpenKey, ncrypt.dll, Passport Key Storage Provider, Platform KSP, JWT assertion, WebAuthn, FIDO2, passkey, roadtx, ROADtools, SigninLogs, KQL, device registration, Conditional Access, ngcmfa, Microsoft Graph, UserAuthenticationMethod.ReadWrite